* When should accessor\nmethods be used?
** Holub (2003):\n"getters/setters are evil"
*** They expose implementation details\n(type & existence of fields)
*** Changing a field type breaks\nevery caller → poor maintainability
*** Leads to "data bags" + procedural code\nthat manipulates objects from outside
*** Ask the object that owns the data\nto do the work, not for its data
*** Prefer behaviour methods:\naccount.deposit(x) instead of setBalance()
** Core OO principle
*** Encapsulation / information hiding
*** Tell, don't ask
*** Law of Demeter
*** Model behaviour (use cases),\nnot data
left side
** When accessors ARE justified
*** Use case really needs the value\n(e.g. display DVD info in cart → getters)
*** Boundary layers: UI, persistence,\nserialization, DTO, JavaBeans frameworks
*** Immutable value objects\n(getter only, no setter)
*** Configuration / simple data holders
** Good practices
*** Fields always private
*** Add getter/setter only when a use case\nrequires it (AIMS: getters yes, setters no)
*** Setters should validate & keep invariants
*** Return copies of mutable fields\n(defensive copy)
*** Prefer meaningful methods:\nisMatch(), totalCost(), toString()
** Other sources
*** Effective Java (Bloch):\nminimize mutability & accessibility
*** Martin Fowler: Tell Don't Ask,\nAnemic Domain Model
*** Clean Code (Martin):\nobjects vs data structures
